Paul E Collins wrote:
> Stephen Jones wrote:
>
> > The monsters move in real time and not in a
> > 'turn' fashion such as in nethack.
>
> Sounds potentially interesting, but I think the above takes it far
> from a "true roguelike".

I disagree with you here.  I think the essence of roguelike games is
tied to the random generation, item ID scrambling and complex monsters.
 Almost all current roguelikes are designed in a way that make
real-time play difficult or unwise to implement, but that doesn't mean
that a game couldn't come along that could make it work.  I actually
can think of one quasi-roguelike that does have real-time play: SSI's
commercial, D&D-licensed Dungeon Hack, which is no Nethack but isn't
too bad of a game really.

